00:00:35.119 --> 00:00:38.840
James, our master of microscopes, 
is not a farmer.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.070
He is, to put it simply, fascinated by microbes.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.320
And that may lead him to strange places and
cause him to grow tanks full of weird things.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.594
But he is not a farmer.

00:00:49.594 --> 00:00:56.594
So why did James, master of microscopes that
he is, decide to put a sweet potato in a tank?

00:00:57.169 --> 00:00:59.919
Well, I guess you could say he was trying
to rescue it.

00:01:00.615 --> 00:01:06.508
This poor, neglected sweet potato had reached
the point of inedibility, with sprouts growing all over it.

00:01:06.508 --> 00:01:10.640
So it seemed right to give the
sweet potato some space of its own,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.170
and let it live a little.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.640
For James, the inevitable conclusion was to
put the sweet potato into a tank.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.500
And here it is, thriving as its sprouts grow
tall and its roots bury deep.

00:01:22.986 --> 00:01:28.630
There are times when James can even spot the
sprouts trying to escape the seal on the tank.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.443
Maybe he would let them burst out, but the
tank is currently full of mosquitos,

00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:36.140
so for now it stays sealed.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.950
The mosquitos are a story for
another day.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:44.980
Today, we’re here for the sweet potato because
it has a very important job, one that it probably

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.580
never anticipated having.

00:01:47.121 --> 00:01:52.170
This sweet potato is going to be a stand-in
for one of the most important chemical and

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:58.151
biological processes in our world: nitrogen
fixation.

00:01:59.396 --> 00:02:02.970
Let’s start by taking a tour of the sweet
potato’s roots.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:07.420
Imagine that instead of the tank water that
they’ve been in, that you’re instead watching

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:11.376
the roots from where they would usually be:
buried in the soil.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:18.779
You might see a scene like this, a crowd of
countless bacteria traveling towards the root

00:02:18.779 --> 00:02:20.311
and away from it.

00:02:20.311 --> 00:02:24.221
Some are shaped like tiny rods, and others
are spherical.

00:02:24.321 --> 00:02:28.981
And some form filaments that trail off like
threads.

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:34.121
As you look around the root, you can see these
bacteria latch onto the root, almost as if

00:02:34.121 --> 00:02:37.452
they’re a part of the plant’s own texture.

00:02:37.452 --> 00:02:42.572
And along the root, you might find organisms like
this Nostoc, a cyanobacteria that can even be

00:02:42.572 --> 00:02:46.372
found on plants like cycads that have above-ground
roots.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:53.829
The clinginess of Nostoc is important to plants—and
actually to all of us—because they can do

00:02:53.829 --> 00:02:55.790
nitrogen fixation.

00:02:57.535 --> 00:03:01.309
What is that? Well it's a concept that comes
up often on our channel.

00:03:01.309 --> 00:03:06.329
We talk about microbes that have an outsized
impact on our world through their role in

00:03:06.329 --> 00:03:12.688
translating atmospheric nitrogen into chemical
forms that plants can actually use.

00:03:12.688 --> 00:03:18.752
But we’ve never really dived into what that
means and why it’s so important.

00:03:18.752 --> 00:03:23.079
It all comes down to the fact that nitrogen
is both essential and abundant,

00:03:23.079 --> 00:03:25.439
and yet difficult to get.

00:03:25.892 --> 00:03:30.947
When we think of the chemistry of life and 
of humans and of our planet’s atmosphere,

00:03:30.947 --> 00:03:35.510
our attention often gets 
focused on oxygen, and carbon-dioxide. 

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:41.453
But nitrogen is the majority of our atmosphere,
and it is extremely important.

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:47.489
Nitrogen is essential to making amino acids
and nucleic acids, and those are important things.

00:03:47.489 --> 00:03:51.000
So if all of the nitrogen in the world were
to disappear in an instant, 

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.717
we would not have protein.

00:03:52.717 --> 00:03:54.626
We would not have DNA.

00:03:54.626 --> 00:03:59.161
We wouldn’t have the fundamental
building blocks that make life on this earth possible.

00:03:59.161 --> 00:04:02.829
Which, for the record, 
I think, would be bad.

00:04:02.829 --> 00:04:05.419
And this is true for all life.

00:04:05.419 --> 00:04:09.109
This is something that we have in 
common with the sweet potato.

00:04:09.109 --> 00:04:11.090
We all need nitrogen.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.815
Fortunately, as I said before, nitrogen is
abundant.

00:04:14.815 --> 00:04:19.186
Around 78% of our atmosphere is made of
nitrogen gas.

00:04:19.385 --> 00:04:20.615
So we should be good.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:21.690
Right?

00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:24.000
Well, no.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.943
The problem is that nitrogen gas is diatomic
nitrogen, two atoms of nitrogen

00:04:28.943 --> 00:04:32.716
bonded together, and that's just far too stable.

00:04:32.716 --> 00:04:37.910
It’s hard for our bodies to break the bonds
between those nitrogens and convert them into

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:44.466
the forms we need, which is why we need eat other
animals and plants that have nitrogen.

00:04:44.466 --> 00:04:47.500
But plants have the same problem as us.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.820
They can’t turn all that nitrogen in the
atmosphere into forms that they can use.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:55.330
But unlike us, plants have a secret weapon.

00:04:55.893 --> 00:05:00.980
The things that we’ve been watching
throughout this video: microbes.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:06.480
These microbes—these nitrogen-fixing microbes—are
special because they have an enzyme called

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.240
nitrogenase.

00:05:08.328 --> 00:05:14.348
This enzyme catalyzes the break-up of that
strong bond holding nitrogen gas together,

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:19.475
freeing up the nitrogens to become ingredients
in more accessible chemicals like ammonia.

00:05:20.481 --> 00:05:27.000
There's one catch though: nitrogenase stops
working completely when it’s exposed to oxygen.

00:05:27.441 --> 00:05:31.727
So nitrogen-fixing organisms have a 
few options for how to live.

00:05:31.727 --> 00:05:36.137
Many of them live in anaerobic conditions,
where the lack of oxygen means their vital

00:05:36.137 --> 00:05:37.847
enzyme will be safe.

00:05:37.847 --> 00:05:42.336
And other organisms, like the cyanobacteria
we’ve been looking at, create their own

00:05:42.336 --> 00:05:48.586
internal forms of protection, sequestering
the nitrogenase into specialized cells called

00:05:48.586 --> 00:05:50.437
heterocysts.

00:05:50.437 --> 00:05:55.840
You can see the heterocysts standing out like
special green beads on this dolichospermum,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.104
which is a kind of cyanobacteria.

00:05:58.104 --> 00:06:01.951
They’re super obvious rounder and larger
than the other cells within

00:06:01.951 --> 00:06:03.790
the cyanobacteria  filament.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:09.610
And they look so different because they need
thick walls so that oxygen can’t pass through,

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.310
which allows the nitrogenase to do its thing.

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:17.150
But these nitrogen-fixing microbes don’t
keep the nitrogen for themselves.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:21.970
Bound to the roots of plants, they give some
of what they’ve made up in exchange for

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.950
other nutrients that the plants have made.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.540
Nitrogen-fixation isn’t the only way that
plants get their nitrogen.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:34.460
There’s also fertilizer, the death and decay
of other living organisms, and even lightning,

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:39.821
which can shatter the bonds of atmospheric
nitrogen and set up its conversion into other forms.

00:06:40.341 --> 00:06:45.000
Like any other element, nitrogen’s journey
through our ecosystems takes many twists and

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.480
turns, setting up delicate balances and potentially
dire consequences.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.680
Too little nitrogen, and life can’t happen.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.660
Too much, and you can choke off lakes
as the thriving plants and algae eventually

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.800
reach the limits of what their ecosystem can
support.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:09.775
It’s this interplay of nitrogen among so
many other elements that births, kills, and sustains.

00:07:09.775 --> 00:07:15.770
It travels through our atmosphere, our soils,
our lakes, our bodies…and ends up, sometimes,

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:22.440
at the root of a strange, rescued sweet potato
sprouting in a mosquito-filled tank.

